  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1034 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nRain/storm chances... Some CAMs are still picking up on a signal \nfor pop-up showers or storms (10 to 20% chance) over the north \nthis afternoon and again Thursday afternoon (20 to 30% chance) due\nto weak instability. However, current thinking is that generating\na shower or storm will be difficult due to lack of forcing and \nsmoke aloft. Overall, most areas should stay dry with upper \nridging firmly in place.\n\nBetter chances for a more widespread wetting rain will come on\nFriday as a potent shortwave spins up a surface low over the\nnorthern Plains. Current long-range solutions show more of a\nglancing blow to our southern tier of counties where higher PWATs\n(1.5 to 1.75\") are holed up closer to the low, though mid-range\nguidance shows pockets of heavier rainfall occurring with better\ninstability and skinny CAPE profiles Friday afternoon. As such, WPC\nhighlights portions of central Wisconsin in a Day 3 slight risk \nfor excessive rainfall. NBM probabilities show a broad 30 to 60% \nsignal for 0.5\" of QPF along/south of Hwy 29. Convective activity \nshould remain fairly limited due to weak shear (< 20 knots). High\npressure then builds into the weekend, foretelling of a drier\nforecast into early next week. \n\nSmoke... GOES satellite imagery shows a plume of elevated wildfire\nsmoke spreading over Wisconsin from north to south early this \nafternoon as high pressure sits and spins over lower Michigan. \nSubsident airmass is expected to bring some of this smoke to the\nsurface at times beginning later this afternoon, becoming more \nwidespread on Thursday. Air quality is forecast to fall into the \nmoderate (yellow) category during this time, with minor reductions\nin visibility possible. Worth noting is that overall air quality \nimpacts are not expected to be as drastic as earlier in the month.",
